1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,309
Lesson 18: VHF stacks, noise and G/T

2
00:00:04,359 --> 00:00:16,035
YA-NEC course, lesson 18: VHF stacks, noise and G/T. Examples: the DK7ZB 10-element Yagi for 144 MHz, 28 ohm.

3
00:00:16,085 --> 00:00:28,205
In free space: 28.1 - j6.1 ohm, 15.41 dBi, F/B 26 dB, beamwidth 30.9 x 33.1 degrees.

4
00:00:28,255 --> 00:00:33,932
Noise/GT/Stack tab: what the antenna hears, not only what it radiates.

5
00:00:33,982 --> 00:00:46,451
Sky at 200 K, ground at 1000 K, antenna aimed 30 degrees up: antenna temperature 264 K, 93 % from the sky, 7 % from the ground through the side and rear lobes.

6
00:00:46,501 --> 00:00:59,057
G/T of the antenna -8.81 dB, as in VE7BQH's tables; with cable loss and a 0.3 dB preamplifier the system G/T is -9.14 dB.

7
00:00:59,107 --> 00:01:14,307
Stack: 2 stacked, 'use the suggested spacings' (DL6WU: 3.65 m), Calculate. The NEC computes the whole stack, coupling included.

8
00:01:14,357 --> 00:01:28,288
18.41 dBi, stack gain +3.0 dB, ideal +3; G/T from -8.81 to -5.96 dB. The antenna temperature rises only 10 K.

9
00:01:28,338 --> 00:01:47,521
Now 2 x 2.

10
00:01:47,571 --> 00:01:59,735
21.44 dBi, +6.03 dB; G/T -2.86 dB, system -3.18 dB: almost 6 dB better than one antenna.

11
00:01:59,785 --> 00:02:19,614
Now long Yagis: DL6WU calculator, 17 elements, open as model.

12
00:02:19,664 --> 00:02:38,766
17 elements: 16.74 dBi in free space, 1.3 dB more than the 10-element short Yagi.

13
00:02:38,816 --> 00:03:09,347
Back to Noise/GT/Stack: 2 x 2 with the suggested spacings.

14
00:03:09,397 --> 00:03:20,150
22.54 dBi, stack gain +5.8 dB, G/T -1.89 dB (one antenna -7.47 dB).

15
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:32,196
Long Yagis have narrower beams: the DL6WU distances grow, and the stack gives slightly less than the ideal 6 dB. VE7BQH's G/T tables use the same method.

16
00:03:32,246 --> 00:03:44,475
Short or long, single or 2 x 2: gain, temperature and G/T together. Next lesson: the loop Yagi for 1296 MHz. 73 de IC8TEM
